>>>>
>>>> On 11/4/13, 9:25 AM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>> I don't think L-BFGS dominates BFGS or vice versa, even within a
>>>>> particular class of optimization problems. Also, you
>>>>> wouldn't necessarily know if the Hessian were ill-conditioned until you
>>>>> solve the problem. In the chapter on limited
>>>>> memory methods, Nocedal and Wright are only comparing L-BFGS to other
>>>>> limited memory methods. I don't remember them ever
>>>>> comparing L-BFGS to BFGS because the main difference between them is that
>>>>> L-BFGS is simply necessary when your
>>>>> optimization problem has so many parameters that the machine doesn't have
>>>>> enough RAM to do BFGS.
>>>>>
>>>>> But in the BFGS chapter
>>>>>
>>>>>
>>>>> http://download.springer.com.ezproxy.cul.columbia.edu/static/pdf/265/chp%253A10.1007%252F978-0-387-40065-5_6.pdf?auth66=1383747383_b6da37dfd810ad1d77e52760cb0ffce8&ext=.pdf
>>>>>
>>>>> they do talk about the ability of BFGS to self-correct within a few
>>>>> iterations if the Hessian approximation strays.
>>>>> Also, at some point during the process, BFGS achieves better than linear
>>>>> convergence on well-behaved problems, whereas I
>>>>> think L-BFGS is linear throughout.
>>>>>
>>>>> Ben
>>>>>
>>>>> On Monday, November 4, 2013 2:34:45 AM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>>      On 11/3/13, 8:42 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:
>>>>>       > Nocedal and Wright
>>>>>       >
>>>>>       >
>>>>>
>>>>> http://download.springer.com.ezproxy.cul.columbia.edu/static/pdf/264/chp%253A10.1007%252F978-0-387-40065-5_7.pdf?auth66=1383700937_ddec5bba9ea5a1994ae00038b73a436a&ext=.pdf
>>>>>
>>>>> <http://download.springer.com.ezproxy.cul.columbia.edu/static/pdf/264/chp%253A10.1007%252F978-0-387-40065-5_7.pdf?auth66=1383700937_ddec5bba9ea5a1994ae00038b73a436a&ext=.pdf>
>>>>>
>>>>>       >
>>>>>       > claims on page 180 that "The main weakness of the L-BFGS method is
>>>>> that it converges slowly on ill-conditioned
>>>>>       > problems�specifically, on problems where the Hessian matrix
>>>>> contains a wide distribution of eigenvalues." This is
>>>>>
>>>>>       > basically the same deal as strong Wolfe vs. weak Wolfe: I don't
>>>>> know how we can presume that the user has a
>>>>>       > well-conditioned / non-degenerate / etc. problem, particularly if
>>>>> someone is using it for some arbitrary
>>>>>      optimization
>>>>>       > problem as opposed to finding a posterior mode or whatever.
>>>>>
>>>>>      Is BFGS better behaved?  I didn't see a direct comparison.
>>>>>      In fact, I can't seem to find much practical discussion anywhere
>>>>>      of plain-old BFGS.
>>>>>
>>>>>      Also, given that we're doing positive-definite Hessian
>>>>>      approximations, is there some connection to what we'll be
>>>>>      doing with Michael's SoftAbs construction for RHMC?
>>>>>
>>>>>       > So, I think we should have options. The documentation of L-BFGS is
>>>>> basically the same as the documentation of
>>>>>      BFGS with
>>>>>       > about 1 additional option.
>>>>>
>>>>>      The effort's not in the original doc or even in the initial
>>>>>      coding, but in the maintenance.
>>>>>
>>>>>      And by that, I mean not only the doc, but also testing of
>>>>>      the algorithm and its interfaces through to command/R/Python,
>>>>>      as well as dealing with questions on the mailing list.
>>>>>
>>>>>      And again, these aren't just write once and they work forever,
>>>>>      any refactoring requires more work again because of the two
>>>>> alternatives.
>>>>>
>>>>>      If BFGS and L-BFGS have different behavior in terms of convergence
>>>>>      or efficiency, we need to doc it so that users know which one to
>>>>>      use.  If one dominates the other, we only need the good one.
>>>>>
>>>>>      I'm not saying we shouldn't have both --- I don't know much
>>>>>      about either.  I'm just trying to point out that multiplying
>>>>>      options is by no means cheap in software we plan to maintain.
>>>>>
>>>>>      - Bob
