> Will this do the right thing for HMC and BFGS?

This is effectively returning a subgradient for points where the gradient isn't defined.  HMC won't care so long as same subgradient is always returned (which it is in this case).  The regions where this will happen are extremely small, so, except for a few pathological cases, I wouldn't expect it to impact either method significantly.

> I'm also OK with just raising an exception or returning an infinite
> gradient.

That could also work, but in some sense it just moves the problem around a bit in terms of things like initialization.

Cheers,
Marcus

