References: <F2YPrJrMiVA.BR7Z-bI9bzgJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <F2YPrJrMiVA.FlU15IOw0sYJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/F2YPrJrMiVA/m/OHr30Keb5hwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There are two options -- either there's not enough mass around zero, chains never hit the singularity, and everything is fine.  But if there is enough mass around zero then the chains can hit the singularity and problems arise.  As Dan noted the fact that people like "0" makes initialization a problem even if sampling isn't.  In either case, the user should be informed because they have an ill-defined gradient being used in their model which is a bad idea™ as it is usually indicative of a modeling (or initialization) mistake.

On Nov 20, 2013, at 6:48 PM, Marcus Brubaker <marcus.a...@gmail.com> wrote:

> If we change the gradient near singularities does it still get stuck?  Throwing an exception effectively punts the problem to the user.  I agree that we should be producing better error better error messages.

