On Thu, Nov 21, 2013 at 3:19 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:

> If these guys aren't in in the next few days the paper is going to be non-trivially compromised.

I don't quite follow. Can't you state that it'll be out in v2.1.0?

>  Given the time at which the pull-requests were made, and the number of additional fixes that were added after that, I don't' think think this is at all unreasonable.

Most of the additional fixes were bug fixes, not changes to behavior in working code. I messed up the process by not creating a hotfix branch right away and we had some minor non-bugfixes in there. But mostly bugs. We want to release to squash bugs that exist in v2.0.1.

Although timing since submission is important, it isn't what I'm prioritizing for a patch release. #352 seems pretty tame, but #356 is a different. That's designed to have a different behavior: 0.8 vs 0.65 as the target acceptance probability. (I didn't realize that this actually had different behavior until Bob reminded me... I must have been too tired when I looked over it.) There are also a bunch of classes that were introduced, but no additional tests, in that pull request. (Another thing I missed.)

So, I think it'll be better to hold these features for the next feature release, v2.1.0, which we can release right after v2.0.2. (By right after, I mean after having those pull requests tested, verified, and pulled in, so another couple days at the soonest?)

So going back to the paper, how will it be compromised? Is it not sufficient to state that it's in v2.1.0?

